---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../styles/ocean-folio.css';
import { OCEAN_FOLIO } from '../../data/ocean-folio.mjs';
import DyeTank from '../../components/DyeTank.astro';
const first=OCEAN_FOLIO[0];
---
<Base wide title="Hidden Rivers | ocean lessons and prints" description="How moving water supports life, with an interactive salinity dye tank, scientific ocean maps, classroom experiments and text-free presentation." pageClass="ocean-folio-page" image="/hidden-rivers/ocean-folio/depth-print-preview.png" imageAlt="Four Agulhas current maps on identical geographic and speed scales.">
  <article class="ocean-folio" data-folio={JSON.stringify(OCEAN_FOLIO)}>
    <header class="folio-header"><a href="/hidden-rivers/">Hidden <em>Rivers</em></a><p>Ocean folio</p><button id="folio-present" type="button">Present</button></header>

    <section class="classroom-opening" id="classroom" aria-labelledby="classroom-title">
      <div class="classroom-intro"><p class="folio-kicker">GRADES 4–8 · A FIVE-MINUTE OCEAN CONVERSATION</p><h1 id="classroom-title">How moving water<br/>supports life</h1><p>Water carries oxygen, nutrients and heat through the ocean. Some of that motion happens far below the waves. A little salt and a drop of dye can help us understand why water sinks.</p><div class="folio-actions"><a href="/hidden-rivers/?story=classroom&amp;chapter=0">Start the five-part map story ↗</a><a href="#saltwater-demo">Try the saltwater demo ↓</a><a href="#scientific-images">Scientific images ↓</a><a href="/hidden-rivers/research/">Research tools ↗</a></div></div>
      <figure><img src="/hidden-rivers/ocean-folio/agulhas-0-preview.png" width="1920" height="1080" alt="Model currents south of Africa bend into loops and rings."/><figcaption>Moving water south of Africa. Gold indicates faster horizontal flow in this HYCOM model image. These colors do not measure oxygen or nutrients.</figcaption></figure>
    </section>
    <section class="classroom-lesson" aria-label="Short classroom lesson">
      <div id="saltwater-cup"><p class="folio-kicker">01 · PREDICT, THEN WATCH · ABOUT TWO MINUTES</p><h2>Will the dyed saltwater sink?</h2><p>Use a clear cup of fresh water, a second cup, table salt, food coloring and a dropper. Let both cups of water reach the same room temperature.</p><ol><li>Dissolve about one teaspoon of salt in 100 mL of water in the second cup. Mix a drop of food coloring into this saltwater.</li><li>Ask: “What will happen when this water meets the fresh water?” Gently release a little dyed saltwater just below the fresh water’s surface. Keep the cup still.</li><li>Watch the colored plume sink and spread. For comparison, add a little dyed <em>fresh</em> water to another cup of fresh water in the same way.</li></ol><p>Salt makes water denser: the same volume has more mass. The dye helps us see the motion. A drop of dye alone is not a saltwater test, and a forceful squirt can move water even without a density difference.</p><details><summary>If you also make a vortex</summary><p>Swirl a separate cup to show rotation. Keep the density demonstration still first, so students can distinguish sinking from spinning. Neither cup reproduces a whole ocean current.</p></details></div>
      <div><p class="folio-kicker">02 · CONNECT TO MELTING ICE · ABOUT ONE MINUTE</p><h2>Fresh water changes the layers</h2><p>Glaciers are made of fresh water. When land ice melts into the sea, it lowers the salt content nearby. At the same temperature, fresher water is less dense than saltier water. A lighter surface layer can make it harder for surface water to mix with deeper water.</p><p>Cold also affects density. Ask: “What would we need to keep the same to test only the effect of salt?” Compare water at the same temperature before discussing melting ice, which changes both temperature and salt content.</p><a href="#teaching">Explore salt, temperature and calculated density ↓</a></div>
      <div><p class="folio-kicker">03 · CONNECT TO LIFE · ABOUT TWO MINUTES</p><h2>Oxygen goes down; nutrients can return</h2><p>Oxygen enters surface water from the air and from photosynthesis. Sinking water and mixing help carry dissolved oxygen into the ocean’s interior. Animals use that oxygen.</p><p>As organisms die and decay, nutrients are released into the water. Upwelling and mixing can bring deeper nutrients toward the sunlit surface. Tiny plants called phytoplankton use nutrients and sunlight to grow, supporting ocean food webs.</p><p>These pathways are connected, but they are not one simple pipe. Winds, tides, density differences, Earth’s rotation and the shape of the seafloor all help shape the motion.</p><details><summary>One question to finish</summary><p>“If the surface and deep ocean mixed less, how might that affect the oxygen reaching animals below and the nutrients reaching plants above?” The effects vary by place; a current map alone cannot measure them.</p></details></div>
    </section>
    <details class="classroom-sources"><summary>For the teacher: what the pictures can tell us</summary><p>The saltwater cup demonstrates a density contrast. Satellite pictures show surface patterns; floats and ship instruments measure properties below the surface. Ocean models combine physics with observations to estimate currents. The maps here do not directly show oxygen or nutrient transport. Tessera compares coastal satellite representations; it does not measure deep currents or oxygen.</p><p><a href="https://oceanexplorer.noaa.gov/ocean-fact/currents/">NOAA: what causes ocean currents ↗</a> · <a href="https://sos.noaa.gov/datasets/ocean-circulation-conveyor-belts/">NOAA: freshwater and circulation ↗</a> · <a href="https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/upwelling.html">NOAA: nutrients and upwelling ↗</a> · <a href="/hidden-rivers/research/">Model methods, Tessera and source data ↗</a></p><p>For grades 4–5, focus on “same space, more mass” and predict what sinks. For grades 6–8, use the freshwater comparison as a control and discuss how temperature and salt can have competing effects.</p></details>
    <nav id="scientific-images" class="folio-chapters" aria-label="Ocean studies">{OCEAN_FOLIO.map((p,i)=><button type="button" data-chapter={i} aria-pressed={i===0}><span>0{i+1}</span>{p.title}</button>)}<a href="#saltwater-demo" id="folio-tank-link"><span>04</span>Dye tank</a></nav>
    <section class="folio-study" aria-label="Selected scientific image">
      <div class="folio-image-wrap"><img id="folio-image" src="/hidden-rivers/ocean-folio/agulhas-0-preview.png" width="1920" height="1080" alt={first.views[0].alt} fetchpriority="high"/><div id="folio-image-status" role="status" hidden>Loading image…</div></div>
      <div class="folio-reading">
        <p id="folio-place" class="folio-kicker">{first.place}</p><h2 id="folio-title">{first.title}</h2><p id="folio-copy">{first.text}</p>
        <div id="folio-views" class="folio-views" role="group" aria-label="Physical quantity or depth">{first.views.map((v,i)=><button type="button" data-view={i} aria-pressed={i===0}>{v.label}</button>)}</div>
        <div class="folio-scale"><p id="folio-quantity">{first.views[0].quantity}</p><img id="folio-ramp" src="/hidden-rivers/ocean-folio/speed-scale.png" width="512" height="16" alt=""/><div id="folio-ticks">{first.views[0].ticks.map(t=><span>{t}</span>)}</div></div>
        <p id="folio-evidence" class="folio-evidence">{first.evidence}</p>
        <div class="folio-actions"><a id="folio-print" href="/hidden-rivers/ocean-folio/depth-print.png" download>Download print ↗</a><a id="folio-visual" href="/hidden-rivers/ocean-folio/agulhas-0.png" download>Image only ↗</a><a id="folio-live" href={first.live}>Explore the current ↗</a></div>
        <details class="folio-details"><summary>Sources and interpretation</summary><p id="folio-limits">{first.limits}</p><div id="folio-sources">{first.sources.map(([label,url])=><a href={url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{label} ↗</a>)}</div><a href="/hidden-rivers/ocean-folio/manifest.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Exact grids, checksums and processing ↗</a><p>Images are exported at 3,840 × 2,160 pixels. Map colors use linear display interpolation within valid source cells; the section retains native cells. The export size does not create finer source detail.</p></details>
      </div>
    </section>
    <p class="folio-archive-link"><a href="/hidden-rivers/research/" target="_top">Ocean calculations, films and earlier maps ↗</a></p>
    <DyeTank />
    <section class="folio-teaching" id="teaching"><div><p class="folio-kicker">FOR A CONVERSATION</p><h2 id="folio-question">{first.question}</h2><details open><summary>Talking points</summary><p id="folio-answer">{first.answer}</p></details></div>
      <div class="density-lab"><h3>Change one water property</h3><p>Seawater density at the surface, calculated with TEOS-10.</p><label>Temperature <output id="density-temperature">10 °C</output><input id="density-t" type="range" min="0" max="30" step="0.25" value="10"/></label><label>Practical salinity <output id="density-salinity">35</output><input id="density-s" type="range" min="30" max="38" step="0.1" value="35"/></label><div class="density-result"><output id="density-value" aria-live="polite">Loading calculation…</output><span>kg/m³ · 0 dbar reference pressure</span></div><p class="density-reference">Salt and temperature change together in the real ocean. This controlled calculation holds pressure and the reference location fixed; it does not simulate sinking or mixing.</p></div>
    </section>
    <section class="folio-research"><p class="folio-kicker">WHAT THE NEXT MAPS NEED TO SHOW</p><h2>Following the water to the seabed</h2><div class="folio-research-grid">
      <div><h3>Denmark Strait: a descending plume</h3><p>A map alone hides the important geometry. Pair the roughly 650 m sill and seabed with ship sections of temperature, salinity, density and velocity at the sill. A separate downstream layer would show descent and mixing, distinguishing its observations from model output. The existing 1,000 m map cannot reconstruct that descent.</p><a href="https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10163193" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sill observations: Lin et al. (2020) ↗</a><br/><a href="https://doi.org/10.1029/2002JC001548" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Downstream observations and model: Käse et al. (2003) ↗</a><p class="folio-research-status">Research direction. Public mooring records are available; a downloadable set of the complete gridded ship sections has not yet been verified.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Ross Sea: the surface signature of deep water</h3><p>Dense shelf water leaves through seabed troughs. A 2025 study used ocean models to test a sea-surface-height proxy, then compared satellite variability with hydrographic evidence. A useful image would put the deep plume, the surface signal and the sampling gaps beside one another.</p><a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-025-02210-7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Altimetry study: Auger et al. (2025) ↗</a><p class="folio-research-status">Research direction. Figure data are openly archived; reconstructing the observation-based proxy requires the along-track altimetry and the published filtering and coverage criteria.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Congo Canyon: sediment makes a gravity current</h3><p>Suspended sediment can make water dense enough to travel down a submarine canyon. Moorings and cable-break times documented a January 2020 current that travelled more than 1,100 km and accelerated along its route. A reconstruction could map those measured arrival times against the canyon.</p><a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-022-31689-3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Measured canyon flows: Talling et al. (2022) ↗</a><p class="folio-research-status">Research direction. Sensor coverage, interval-averaged front speeds and multibeam coverage must remain visible in a reconstruction.</p></div>
    </div><p class="folio-article">Denmark Strait carries a bottom-following overflow, and the Gulf Stream also has a major wind-driven component. Satellites measure surface signals; they do not photograph deep currents. Popular descriptions of underwater waterfalls and conveyor belts simplify these different processes. <a href="https://discoverwildscience.com/the-oceans-hidden-rivers-how-water-flows-beneath-the-sea-2-373648/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Introductory overview ↗</a> · <a href="https://www.whoi.edu/ocean-learning-hub/ocean-facts/will-the-gulf-stream-really-shut-down/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WHOI's explanation ↗</a></p></section>
    <div class="folio-present-controls" hidden><button id="folio-prev" type="button" aria-label="Previous visual">←</button><button id="folio-pause" type="button" aria-label="Pause or play the tank">Ⅱ</button><button id="folio-next" type="button" aria-label="Next visual">→</button><button id="folio-exit" type="button" aria-label="Exit presentation">×</button></div>
  </article>
  <script>import {startOceanFolio} from '../../scripts/hidden-rivers/ocean-folio.js';startOceanFolio();</script>
</Base>
